# Part 1: Classical Decomposition (+150 words)
m.day44_lesson["parts"][0]["say"].extend([
    "When computing moving averages for time series with an even period length (such as 4 quarters or 12 months), a simple moving average falls directly between two calendar periods rather than aligning with an exact time point. To resolve this geometric misalignment, quantitative analysts compute a Centered Moving Average (CMA)—specifically, a 2x4 Moving Average for quarterly data or a 2x12 Moving Average for monthly data. This two-stage smoothing first averages adjacent cycles and then centers the resulting trend line directly over historical calendar periods.",
    "Furthermore, understanding whether a business time series is Additive or Multiplicative determines how seasonal indices are scaled. In an additive model, seasonal indices represent absolute unit increments that sum to zero across the cycle. In a multiplicative model, seasonal indices represent percentage multipliers (e.g., Q1 = 0.85, Q4 = 1.35) whose arithmetic mean is exactly 1.0. Applying an additive model to a rapidly growing business that exhibits expanding percentage seasonal surges will systematically under-predict holiday demand as the company scales."
])

# Part 2: Holt-Winters ETS (+120 words)
